        {
            "number": 6,
            "revision": "fae2422bda90ce7bd52e951c2a15a715c78f5a3d",
            "parents": [
                "4fa796cb89641b3db4b4b44ef15ebbb249b1e984"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/69/79569/6",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Anupama B R",
                "email": "anupama.b.r1@ibm.com",
                "username": "branupama"
            },
            "createdOn": 1744622083,
            "author": {
                "name": "Anupama B R",
                "email": "anupama.b.r1@ibm.com",
                "username": "branupama"
            },
            "kind": "NO_CODE_CHANGE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "configuration/ibm/correlated_properties.json",
                    "line": 6,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "priyaram",
                        "email": "priyangaramasamy2@gmail.com",
                        "username": "PriyangaRamasamy"
                    },
                    "message": "with this approach, I need to have 4 for loops. one for service name section, second one for list of interfaces, third one for list of properties, 4th one is for list of paths Pair. which adds up to O(n^4) at the worst case. Do you have any better approach to reduce time complexity?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "configuration/ibm/correlated_properties.json",
                    "line": 6,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Anupama B R",
                        "email": "anupama.b.r1@ibm.com",
                        "username": "branupama"
                    },
                    "message": "As we discussed, this is not taking O(n^4) for search in JSON."
                },
                {
                    "file": "configuration/ibm/correlated_properties.json",
                    "line": 132,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "priyaram",
                        "email": "priyangaramasamy2@gmail.com",
                        "username": "PriyangaRamasamy"
                    },
                    "message": "record VR10, property DC is present only for BMC and base panel only. why it's there for chassis and motherboard?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "configuration/ibm/correlated_properties.json",
                    "line": 132,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Anupama B R",
                        "email": "anupama.b.r1@ibm.com",
                        "username": "branupama"
                    },
                    "message": "VR10 handle is removed for this commit. Will handle this in seperately."
                },
                {
                    "file": "configuration/ibm/correlated_properties.json",
                    "line": 145,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "priyaram",
                        "email": "priyangaramasamy2@gmail.com",
                        "username": "PriyangaRamasamy"
                    },
                    "message": "While processing this json, how to distinguish between \"pathsPair\"(which is a constant section) and  \"com.ibm.ipzvpd.VINI\" (which is a variable section) keys?\n\n\nto elaborate it : \nat runtime, both \"pathsPair\" and \"com.ibm.ipzvpd.VINI\" are looked equally.\none way to decide is based on the value type. if the current key's value type is a json object - consider it as pathsPair.\nif the current key's value type is a string - consider is as interface: property pair.\n\n*in this way we are strictly restricting that only one pair of any unique type can exist.*\n\n\nImpact of this restriction:\nsay I want to add \" encoding type \" : \"DATE\"- where encoding type is not required to add under each pathPair as it is an attribute which describes about the Property BuildDate.\n\nwith this approach i cannot have \" encoding type \" : \"DATE\" as an individual entry under \"BuildDate\".\n\nsame goes with another attribute say \n\"dbusSignature\" : \"s\"  ----> all Asset properties are in string type in Dbus\n\n\"dbusSignature : \"ay\".  -----> all VPD record's keyword are in array of bytes in Dbus\n\nhere if i need to include dbusSignature as an attribute which describes the property type, i cannot have it as an individual attribute under the \"Property\" section because it violates the restriction which I have mentioned above.\n\n\nDo you have any better approach to distinguish between \"pathsPair\" and \"interface\" property\" section at runtime without having any type restrictions in json?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "configuration/ibm/correlated_properties.json",
                    "line": 145,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Anupama B R",
                        "email": "anupama.b.r1@ibm.com",
                        "username": "branupama"
                    },
                    "message": "Will have defaultInterfaces key for path no need to match with pathPair.\nencoding will be handled in separate commit."
                },
                {
                    "file": "configuration/ibm/correlated_properties.json",
                    "line": 278,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "priyaram",
                        "email": "priyangaramasamy2@gmail.com",
                        "username": "PriyangaRamasamy"
                    },
                    "message": "I understand,\n/ebmc_card_bmc - is the name of bmc in rainier machines and\n/bmc is the name of bmc in everest machines.\n\neven though we already have a machine specific VPD config JSONs, we still end up creating a new json with all machine specific configurations :)\nWhich means we are going to handle feature specific JSONs as well here..\n\n\ncan you state a strong reason why this approach is chosen as better one rather than updating the existing machine specific jsons, even though you have dependency with machine specific configurations ?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "configuration/ibm/correlated_properties.json",
                    "line": 278,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Anupama B R",
                        "email": "anupama.b.r1@ibm.com",
                        "username": "branupama"
                    },
                    "message": "as we discussed, base JSON won't be updated instead correlated JSON will be used for maintaining correlation among properties."
                }
            ],
